\fs32\b Face and Eye Detection Project Report\b0\fs24\par
\par
This project combines Django, OpenCV, QR generation and a mobile-friendly workflow to register people, validate a face and both eyes, generate a QR identity, and retrieve a profile after scanning.\par
\par
\b Objectives\b0\par
- Detect a human face and eyes from an uploaded or captured image.\par
- Register a person with name, address, unique number and face image.\par
- Generate a unique QR code linked to the registered profile.\par
- Maintain registration and scan history.\par
- Provide REST API endpoints for person data and face validation.\par
\par
\b Technologies\b0\par
Python - main programming language.\par
Django - backend web framework.\par
Django REST Framework - REST APIs.\par
OpenCV - face and eye detection.\par
SQLite - development database.\par
qrcode and Pillow - QR image generation.\par
HTML, CSS and JavaScript - dashboard and browser camera workflow.\par
Cloudflared - HTTPS tunnel for mobile access.\par
\par
\b Project Structure\b0\par
backend/ - Django project and users app.\par
frontend/ - standalone frontend page.\par
src/ - OpenCV command-line and GUI tools.\par
tests/ - OpenCV cascade tests.\par
android/ - Android sample and setup guide.\par
scripts/ - project report generators.\par
docs/ - generated reports.\par
\par
\b Working Flow\b0\par
1. User opens the Django dashboard.\par
2. User enters name, address and unique number.\par
3. User captures or uploads a face image.\par
4. OpenCV checks for a face and both eyes.\par
5. The person is stored in SQLite.\par
6. A QR code is generated for the profile URL.\par
7. Scanning the QR opens the profile and records scan history.\par
\par
\b Important Commands\b0\par
python3 -m venv .venv\par
source .venv/bin/activate\par
pip install -r requirements.txt\par
python backend/manage.py migrate\par
python backend/manage.py runserver\par
AUTO_TUNNEL=1 FORCE_RESTART=1 APP_PORT=8001 bash run_mobile_server.sh\par
pytest\par
\par
\b Advantages\b0\par
- Simple student/project implementation.\par
- Combines computer vision, web development and QR workflows.\par
- Supports browser camera capture and mobile profile access.\par
- Keeps registration and QR scan history.\par
\par
\b Limitations\b0\par
- Haar cascades are less accurate than modern deep-learning detectors.\par
- Detection depends on lighting, camera quality and face angle.\par
- SQLite is mainly suitable for development and small deployments.\par
- Production use should add stronger authentication, privacy and security controls.\par
\par
\b Conclusion\b0\par
The system demonstrates a complete face-and-eye detection workflow integrated with Django and QR identity generation. It is suitable for learning, demonstrations and prototype identity-registration applications.\par
